---
# K3S, WATCHED FROM TWO PLACES THAT FAIL INDEPENDENTLY.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO VANTAGE POINTS, AND WHY THE FILE IS IN TWO GROUPS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   `k3s`                 node_exporter's systemd collector, scraped over the private network from
#                         OUTSIDE the cluster. It sees one thing -- whether `k3s.service` is
#                         running -- and it keeps seeing it when the cluster itself is unusable.
#   `kube-state-metrics`  kube-state-metrics, running IN the cluster and scraped over a NodePort
#                         (movies-gitops/kube-state-metrics/). It turns the state of Kubernetes
#                         OBJECTS into metrics: node conditions, Deployment replica counts, pod
#                         waiting reasons, container termination reasons.
#
# THE SECOND GROUP IS NEW. This file previously said at length that kube-state-metrics did not
# exist here and that writing node-condition or workload rules against metric names nothing
# produced would be strictly worse than leaving them out -- a rule whose series never exists does
# not error and does not fire, it sits in the rule list looking like coverage. That reasoning has
# not changed; what changed is that the series exist now.
#
# WHAT MAKES THEM ORDER-DEPENDENT RATHER THAN REDUNDANT. kube-state-metrics learns everything from
# the apiserver, so when the control plane is gone it goes quiet -- and quiet is not an alert.
# `K3sServerNotRunning` in the first group is what covers that, from a machine outside the cluster,
# and it is why the systemd rules are not superseded by the richer ones below. Read the `k3s` group
# first when both fire: a node reported NotReady by an apiserver that is itself failing is a
# symptom.
#
# STILL NOT COVERED, so its absence does not read as an oversight: there is no metrics-server and
# no kubelet or cAdvisor scrape, so nothing here knows actual CPU or memory USE per pod --
# `kube_pod_container_resource_requests` is what was ASKED for, not what is consumed. The host-level
# view in host-health.rules covers the machine; attributing pressure to a pod does not.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT CAN BE SAID FROM HERE, AND IT IS NOT NOTHING
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# node_exporter's systemd collector runs on both machines and reports `k3s.service` -- NixOS names
# the unit `k3s.service` for the server and the agent alike, which is why the two rules below are
# separated by the `role` scrape label rather than by unit name. A dead k3s unit is the single
# cause behind almost every "node NotReady" this fleet could plausibly have, so watching the unit
# recovers most of the value of the node-condition rule that cannot be written.
#
# THE SEVERITIES ARE DELIBERATELY LOPSIDED, and that is a statement about what this cluster is FOR
# today: the control plane on monitoring-1 is critical because losing it means no cluster at all
# and no way to schedule the recovery; the agent on k3s-worker-1 is a warning because it currently
# runs NOTHING -- k3s-worker-1 is idle capacity bought ahead of the workloads that will move onto
# it. WHEN REAL WORK RUNS THERE, THE AGENT RULE BECOMES CRITICAL AND THIS PARAGRAPH IS WRONG.
groups:
  - name: k3s
    rules:
      - alert: K3sServerNotRunning
        # SELECTED BY `role`, NOT BY HOSTNAME. `role="monitoring"` is a target label from the
        # `node` scrape job (roles/prometheus.nix generates it from `fleet.prometheus.nodeTargets`),
        # so this rule follows the control plane if it is ever rebuilt on a different machine.
        # A hostname literal would keep pointing at the old box and go quiet.
        expr: node_systemd_unit_state{name="k3s.service",state="active",role="monitoring"} == 0
        # FIVE MINUTES. k3s restarts itself on a config change and takes tens of seconds to come
        # back; five minutes is past that and still names a control plane that is genuinely gone
        # within the hour.
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "The k3s control plane is not running on {{ $labels.host }}"
          description: >-
            No API server: nothing can be scheduled, `kubectl` does not answer, and the agent on
            k3s-worker-1 will go NotReady on its own once its lease expires. `journalctl -u k3s -n
            200`. REMEMBER WHERE THIS PROCESS SITS IN THE QUEUE: k3s runs on the monitoring host
            with `CPUWeight = 100` against Prometheus's 400 and Alertmanager's 300
            (roles/k3s-server.nix says so on its own unit, roles/prometheus.nix on the others), so
            a starved monitoring-1 sheds k3s FIRST, by design. If this fires alongside
            `CpuBusySustained` or a memory alert on the same host, the CPU weighting worked and k3s
            is the symptom rather than the cause.

      - alert: K3sAgentNotRunning
        expr: node_systemd_unit_state{name="k3s.service",state="active",role="k3s-worker"} == 0
        # TEN MINUTES, longer than the server's, because nothing is waiting on this one today.
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "The k3s agent is not running on {{ $labels.host }}"
          description: >-
            A WARNING ONLY BECAUSE THIS NODE IS IDLE. k3s-worker-1 is an 8-core cx43 bought ahead
            of the workloads that will move onto it, and it currently runs none -- so an agent that
            is down costs nothing today. The moment anything real is scheduled there, raise this to
            critical and shorten the `for:`; leaving a warning on a node that serves traffic is how
            an outage gets found by a user. Check that the control plane is up first (see
            `K3sServerNotRunning`): an agent whose server is gone stops for a reason that is not
            its own.

      - alert: K3sUnitStateAbsent
        # ONE `absent()` FOR BOTH, rather than one per host: the two rules above are comparisons
        # and neither can fire without this series, so its disappearance is a single fact about
        # this fleet's ability to see k3s at all. Which host lost it is the first thing
        # `TargetDown` in monitoring-self.rules will already have said, if the cause is a host
        # dropping out of scrape.
        expr: absent(node_systemd_unit_state{name="k3s.service"})
        # THIRTY MINUTES, matching the other `absent()` companions in this directory. Long enough
        # to cover a reboot of both machines; a rule about a rule does not need to be quick.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Nothing is reporting the state of any k3s unit"
          description: >-
            Both k3s alerts are comparisons and cannot fire without this series, so the only view
            of k3s that survives the control plane being gone has itself gone. kube-state-metrics
            does NOT cover this: it learns everything from the apiserver, so it goes quiet in
            exactly the case these two rules exist for. The likely causes are node_exporter's
            systemd collector being off, or both k3s hosts having dropped out of scrape.
            IT DOES NOT MEAN K3S IS RUNNING.

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # THE OBJECT-STATE GROUP. Everything here needs kube-state-metrics to be running IN the cluster
  # and reachable on its NodePort; see movies-gitops/kube-state-metrics/deployment.yaml for the
  # exposure decision and what it costs.
  #
  # THESE SERIES CARRY NO `host` OR `role` LABEL, deliberately, and the scrape job is configured
  # that way on purpose (see `kubeStateMetricsYaml` in roles/prometheus.nix): the address scraped is
  # whichever node answers the NodePort, while the DATA describes the whole cluster. Use the series'
  # own `node`, `namespace`, `pod` and `container` labels; a `host` label here would be true of the
  # connection and false of everything it carried.
  #
  # THE METRIC NAMES ARE kube-state-metrics v2.14.0's, pinned in the Deployment. They are stable
  # across 2.x but not guaranteed across a major bump, and a renamed series is SILENT -- hence the
  # `absent()` companion at the end of this group, which is the one rule here that catches a wrong
  # name, a failed RBAC grant and a dead exporter alike.
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - name: kube-state-metrics
    rules:
      - alert: K3sNodeNotReady
        # `== 0` ON THE `status="true"` SERIES, NOT `== 1` ON `status="false"`. kube-state-metrics
        # publishes three series per condition -- true, false and unknown -- and a node whose
        # kubelet has stopped reporting sits at `unknown`, not `false`. Asserting on the true series
        # covers both bad states and any third one a future version invents.
        expr: kube_node_status_condition{condition="Ready",status="true"} == 0
        # TEN MINUTES. The node lease expires after about 40 seconds and the controller marks a node
        # NotReady shortly after, so this is well past a transient blip -- and past an agent restart
        # during a deploy, which is the common cause of a brief NotReady on this fleet. It is
        # deliberately longer than `K3sServerNotRunning`'s five: when the control plane goes, every
        # node goes NotReady as a consequence, and the cause should page first.
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Kubernetes node {{ $labels.node }} is not Ready"
          description: >-
            CHECK `K3sServerNotRunning` AND `K3sAgentNotRunning` FIRST. This rule reads the
            apiserver's opinion; those two read the machines directly, and a node that is NotReady
            because its k3s unit is dead is one fact reported twice. A node NotReady with both of
            those silent is the interesting case -- the unit is running and the kubelet is failing
            its own checks, for which `journalctl -u k3s -n 200` on that node is the first command.
            SEVERITY IS A WARNING BECAUSE OF WHAT IS SCHEDULED HERE TODAY, matching
            `K3sAgentNotRunning`'s reasoning in the group above; if that rule is ever raised to
            critical, raise this one in the same commit.

      - alert: K3sDeploymentReplicasMissing
        # SPEC MINUS READY, NOT `available`. `available` counts pods past their minReadySeconds,
        # which for a Deployment with no readiness probe is a weaker statement than `ready`.
        expr: >-
          kube_deployment_spec_replicas - kube_deployment_status_replicas_ready > 0
        # FIFTEEN MINUTES, and it is the `for:` that makes this rule usable rather than the
        # expression. Every ordinary deploy passes through this state: a `Recreate` strategy (which
        # is what kinowo/worker-pl uses) is briefly at zero ready by design, and an image pull from
        # a private GHCR on a cold node is minutes on its own. Fifteen minutes is past all of that
        # and still names a rollout that is genuinely stuck within the hour. A GUESS, sized off the
        # slowest normal case rather than measured.
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Deployment {{ $labels.namespace }}/{{ $labels.deployment }} has {{ $value }} replica(s) short"
          description: >-
            A rollout that has not converged. `kubectl -n {{ $labels.namespace }} describe
            deployment {{ $labels.deployment }}` and then `describe pod` on the pending replica; the
            three causes worth ruling out in order are an image that cannot be pulled (the
            `ghcr-pull` secret is a read:packages token created out of band and is not in git -- see
            movies-gitops/worker/README.md), a PersistentVolumeClaim that cannot bind, and a pod
            that cannot be scheduled at all. THAT LAST ONE IS A REAL CONSTRAINT ON THIS CLUSTER, not
            a hypothetical: monitoring-1 is tainted NoSchedule as the control plane, so there is one
            schedulable node, and a pod holding a local-path PVC cannot move off it.

      - alert: K3sPodCrashLooping
        expr: kube_pod_container_status_waiting_reason{reason="CrashLoopBackOff"} == 1
        # TEN MINUTES. The kubelet's backoff reaches its five-minute cap after about six restarts,
        # so ten minutes means the container has failed repeatedly rather than once badly. Shorter
        # than that and this fires during the ordinary first-boot failure of a misconfigured deploy,
        # which the person deploying is already watching.
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.namespace }}/{{ $labels.pod }} container {{ $labels.container }} is in CrashLoopBackOff"
          description: >-
            The container starts, exits, and is restarted with increasing backoff. `kubectl -n
            {{ $labels.namespace }} logs {{ $labels.pod }} -c {{ $labels.container }} --previous` is
            the command that shows WHY -- the current logs are from the attempt that has not failed
            yet. IF THIS IS THE WORKER, note that its own startup path is slow by design (a generous
            startupProbe covers a Mongo hydrate that can take minutes) and that
            `-XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError` is set, so a heap problem presents as a clean exit and a
            restart loop rather than as an OOM kill. Check `K3sContainerOOMKilled` below to tell the
            two apart.

      - alert: K3sContainerOOMKilled
        # TWO CONDITIONS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS WHAT MAKES THIS RULE HONEST.
        # `kube_pod_container_status_last_terminated_reason` describes the LAST termination
        # whenever it happened, so on its own it would go on firing for days about a single kill
        # that has already been dealt with. Gating it on a restart within the same window means it
        # fires about a kill that is HAPPENING, not one that once did.
        expr: >-
          kube_pod_container_status_last_terminated_reason{reason="OOMKilled"} == 1
            and on (namespace, pod, container)
              rate(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total[15m]) > 0
        # FIVE MINUTES, short because this is already a statement about something that has happened
        # rather than a threshold being approached. The 15m rate window above is where the patience
        # lives.
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.namespace }}/{{ $labels.pod }} container {{ $labels.container }} was OOMKilled"
          description: >-
            THE CGROUP LIMIT KILLED IT, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS THE NODE RUNNING OUT OF MEMORY --
            k3s-worker-1 has 16GB and one workload. Check the container's `resources.limits.memory`
            first; the limit is the number that decided this. FOR A JVM CONTAINER, an OOM kill means
            the process grew past its limit in NATIVE memory rather than heap, because
            `-XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError` makes a heap exhaustion a clean exit instead -- so look at
            metaspace, direct buffers and thread stacks, not `-Xmx`. If the host itself is under
            memory pressure, `MemoryAvailableLow` in host-health.rules will be firing alongside
            this and it is the one to read first.

      - alert: KubeStateMetricsSeriesAbsent
        # ONE COMPANION FOR THE WHOLE GROUP, on the series that cannot legitimately be missing: this
        # cluster always has at least one node, so `kube_node_info` is absent only if
        # kube-state-metrics is not being scraped, is not permitted to list nodes, or has renamed
        # the series. The workload series are NOT usable for this -- `kube_deployment_spec_replicas`
        # is legitimately absent on a cluster with no Deployments, so an `absent()` on it would fire
        # on an empty cluster and teach everyone to ignore it.
        expr: absent(kube_node_info)
        # THIRTY MINUTES, matching every other `absent()` companion in this directory.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Nothing is reporting Kubernetes object state"
          description: >-
            Every rule in this group is a comparison and none of them can fire without
            kube-state-metrics, so the cluster's workloads are currently unwatched -- node
            conditions, replica counts, crash loops and OOM kills all included. If `TargetDown`
            is silent alongside this, the exporter IS answering and the problem is inside it:
            either the ClusterRole in movies-gitops/kube-state-metrics/rbac.yaml no longer grants
            `nodes`, or the `--resources=` list in the Deployment stopped naming them, or the image
            was bumped across a rename. Those two files are written to agree with each other and
            nothing enforces it. IT DOES NOT MEAN THE CLUSTER IS HEALTHY.
